WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.858
Timeline and transfer analysis attempts to reconstruct relevant activity across the removable media and host systems.

2
00:00:07.858 --> 00:00:23.035
A useful timeline may include device connection times, mount events, user logons, file creation and modification times, recent file references, application activity, endpoint telemetry, DLP alerts, and network or cloud records.

3
00:00:23.035 --> 00:00:26.194
The goal is sequence, context, and consistency.

4
00:00:26.194 --> 00:00:29.584
Copied versus moved file interpretation is tricky.

5
00:00:29.584 --> 00:00:36.748
A copied file may preserve original modification time while receiving a new creation time on the destination.

6
00:00:36.748 --> 00:00:41.140
A moved file within the same file system may preserve more metadata.

7
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:47.380
A file extracted from an archive may receive timestamps from the archive or extraction process.

8
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:52.773
Different tools and operating systems can produce different timestamp patterns.

9
00:00:52.773 --> 00:00:56.317
Time zones and clock drift can complicate the picture.

10
00:00:56.317 --> 00:01:04.637
A removable device may be examined on one system, created on another, and mounted by a third system with different time settings.

11
00:01:04.637 --> 00:01:08.951
Application records may store UTC, local time, or custom formats.

12
00:01:08.951 --> 00:01:16.193
Reports should preserve original timestamps where appropriate and explain how normalization was performed.

13
00:01:16.193 --> 00:01:19.737
User activity correlation helps avoid overclaiming.

14
00:01:19.737 --> 00:01:33.450
If host artifacts show a device connection, recent documents show file interaction, and media timestamps show new files created during the same window, the combined evidence may support a transfer hypothesis.

15
00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:38.612
If the only evidence is a connection record, the conclusion should be narrower.

16
00:01:38.612 --> 00:01:43.311
Correlation raises confidence; it does not remove the need for caution.

17
00:01:43.311 --> 00:01:46.701
A defensible timeline includes uncertainty notes.

18
00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:56.716
It should say which events are observed directly, which are inferred, which are approximate, and which depend on tool interpretation or system behavior.

19
00:01:56.716 --> 00:02:05.576
Strong timelines help investigators communicate what likely happened without pretending that every timestamp tells the whole story.
